-- title:
--   A pure central braid is a power of the full twist
-- statement:
--   The open direction of the centre computation, with purity available as a hypothesis.
--
--   For $n \ge 3$, let $b$ be a central braid which is moreover pure, that is, $\pi(b) = 1$ where $\pi$ is the underlying-permutation homomorphism. Then $b$ is a power of the full twist $\Delta^2 = (\sigma_1 \cdots \sigma_{n-1})^n$.
--
--   The purity hypothesis is not an extra assumption in disguise: it is automatic. `BraidsLinksMCG.center_le_ker_perm` is proved and says every central braid is pure when $n \ge 3$, the argument being that $\pi$ is surjective, so a central braid has central image, and the symmetric group on at least three letters has trivial centre. Stating it here explicitly is what makes this the form the classical proof actually wants to consume.
--
--   Why that matters. The standard route to the centre works inside the pure braid group $P_n$, where the Fadell--Neuwirth splitting gives the semidirect decomposition $P_n \cong F_{n-1} \rtimes P_{n-1}$ and hence the combing normal form. A central element, written in combed form, is forced to have trivial free part at each stage, and what survives is a power of the full twist. None of that machinery is available for a braid whose permutation is unknown, so any attempt on the statement without the purity hypothesis has to begin by establishing it.
--
--   The alternative route, via the Artin representation, also benefits: for pure $b$ the images satisfy $\xi(b)(x_j) = A_j x_j A_j^{-1}$ with the *same* index on both sides, since $\pi(b) = 1$. Commuting with each $\mathrm{artinEndo}$ then constrains the conjugators $A_j$ to be a common power of $x_1 \cdots x_n$, which is precisely what the full twist induces. Concluding by that route additionally needs faithfulness, `BraidsLinksMCG.artin_representation_injective`.
--
--   The hypothesis $n \ge 3$ is genuinely required and is not carried along for safety. For $n = 2$ the braid group is abelian, so its centre is all of $B_2 \cong \mathbb{Z}$, generated by $\sigma_1$, while the full twist there is $\sigma_1^2$ and generates only an index-two subgroup. The conclusion is therefore false at $n = 2$, and any proof must use $n \ge 3$ somewhere.
-- source:
--   Birman, Braids, Links and Mapping Class Groups, Chapter 1, Corollary 1.8.4; Chow, On the algebraical braid group, Ann. of Math. 49 (1948).

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BraidsLinksMCG_ArtinBraidGroup
import Definitions.Def_BraidsLinksMCG_ArtinEndo

namespace BraidsLinksMCG

theorem pure_center_le_zpowers_full_twist (n : ℕ) (hn : 3 ≤ n)
    (pi : ArtinBraidGroup n →* Equiv.Perm (Fin n))
    (hpi : ∀ i : Fin (n - 1), pi (sigma i) = Equiv.swap (strandIdx i) (strandIdxSucc i))
    (b : ArtinBraidGroup n) (hb : b ∈ Subgroup.center (ArtinBraidGroup n))
    (hpure : pi b = 1) :
    b ∈ Subgroup.zpowers (sigmaProd n ^ n) := by sorry

end BraidsLinksMCG
